microtun-firmware-signer for Debian
====================================

The package intentionally installs the systemd unit disabled and does not start
it during installation. The installed /etc/microtun-firmware-signer/config.toml
is an example configuration and must be reviewed before first use.

The service runs as the dedicated microtun-firmware-signer user and group. If an
encrypted signing key is stored under /etc/microtun-firmware-signer, a typical
secure ownership/mode is:

  root:microtun-firmware-signer 0640

The application also enforces that encrypted key files are not group-writable
and are not accessible by other users.

The packaged systemd unit contains example LoadCredentialEncrypted= directives
for the single signing-key passphrase and GitHub OAuth secret; their credential
names match the example configuration. Deployment-specific
credential mappings should normally be placed in a systemd drop-in instead of
editing the packaged unit, for example:

  /etc/systemd/system/microtun-firmware-signer.service.d/credentials.conf

After configuring the TOML, signing key file, and systemd credentials:

  systemctl daemon-reload
  systemctl enable --now microtun-firmware-signer.service
  systemctl status microtun-firmware-signer.service

The binary is installed as /usr/bin/microtun-firmware-signer.
